<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taha Hussein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ṭāhā Ḥusayn, The Dean of Arabic Literature</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taha Hussein was an Egyptian writer, a leading figure of the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a> in the Arab world, known as the Dean of Arabic Literature. Blind from the age of two, he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-azhar">Al-Azhar</a> and the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> University, then at Montpellier and the Sorbonne, which awarded him a doctorate on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> in 1917. His On Pre-Islamic Poetry (1926), which doubted the authenticity of much early Arabic poetry, angered religious scholars, and he lost his post at Cairo University in 1931. An Egyptian nationalist who rejected pan-Arabism, he became Minister of Education in 1950 and called for free education; in the West he is best known for his autobiography Al-Ayyam (The Days).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taha-hussein.jpg" alt="Taha Hussein" width="233" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Zerida at en.wikipedia (Original text: Zerida) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TahaHussein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 20th century, Arabic literature produced writers such as <b>Taha Hussein</b>, the poet Mahmoud Darwish and the novelist Naguib Mahfouz, who won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1988.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Middle East liberal reform helped create a sense of crisis within Islam that led to Islamic revivalism, yet writers such as <b>Taha Hussein</b> went on advocating liberal values.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maghagha, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Maghagha, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.65,30.84&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Maghagha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5356 1115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Taha Hussein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5806 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5806 1385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.65,30.84&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
